From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Cui <bh@bugs.cc>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 05:26:42 -0400
Subject: feat: open Claude Desktop as a stored account, one window per account

Claude Desktop keeps one login per profile: to use a second account the
user signs out, types the email again, waits for the code, and the first
account is gone. cswap already knows every account, but only for Claude
Code; nothing lets the desktop app follow the same table.

The app is Electron, and Electron honors `--user-data-dir`, so a
separate profile directory is a separate, independently signed-in copy
of the app (the approach of siddhjagani/guise). `cswap desktop
<num|email ...>` gives each stored account a permanent profile under
`<backup>/desktop/<num>-<email slug>/` and opens the app against it
(`open -n -a Claude.app --args --user-data-dir=<dir>` on macOS, the
executable detached elsewhere). The first open shows a sign-in; after
that the account stays signed in, and several accounts can be open side
by side. A window is found again by the exact `--user-data-dir=<dir>`
token on its command line, so a second open brings it forward instead
of duplicating it; `--quit` sends TERM, then KILL after a timeout; a
bare `cswap desktop` lists which accounts are open. Nothing reads,
copies or migrates a login: Claude owns everything in the profile.

The app is looked up in the stock install location (`/Applications` or
`~/Applications` on macOS, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\AnthropicClaude` on Windows)
or the new `desktop.appPath` setting; Linux has no official build and
says so. Removing an account refuses while its window is open and
otherwise deletes the profile with the slot, and `purge` refuses while
any window is open and lists the profiles it removes, the same way both
treat session profiles.

